          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            What may be called
            %em Borel-equivariant rational homotopy theory
            (this is not an established term, but it fits well) is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rational homotopy theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-spaces"} G-spaces
            regarded in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+model+structure"} Borel equivariant homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            are simply the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetting
            the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+action"} group action
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalences"} weak homotopy equivalences
            = succeed "-wise" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+locus"} fixed locus
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fine+model+structure+on+topological+G-spaces"} proper equivariant homotopy theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+rational+homotopy+theory"} proper equivariant rational homotopy theory
          %p
            Borel-equivariant rational homotopy theory is relevant even and particularly to the study of plain (non-equivariant)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rationalization"} rationalization
            beyond the constraint of these being
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nilpotent+topological+space"} nilpotent
            or even
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected+topological+space"} simply connected
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rational homotopy theory
          %p
            Namely, given any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+topological+space"} connected
            space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (but the same discussion applies to each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+component"} connected component
            of a non-connected space), without any restriction on its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fiber+sequence"} homotopy fiber sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mi X
                            %mo ^
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\widehat X
                  \&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;
                  X
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  \\big\downarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  B \pi_1(X)
                  }
          %p with
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+cover"} universal cover
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi X
                          %mo ^
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat X
                %em is
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected+topological+space"} simply connected
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \pi_1(X)
                of its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X)
          %p
            Now, by the general discussion at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-action"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                \-action
            and at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+model+structure"} Borel model structure
            (
            %a{:href => "Borel+model+structure#RelationToSliceOverSimplicialClassifyingSpace"}> here
            ) – this means that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            regarded in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} slice
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mi π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \pi
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} equivalent
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+space"} covering space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat X
            %em equipped
            with its canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+action"} group action
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
          %p
            This allows to regard the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rationalization"} rationalization
            of non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nilpotent+spaces"} nilpotent spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as being the image of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+covers"} universal covers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat X
            in
            = succeed "-eqjuivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X)
            = succeed "-action" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+action"} free
          %p
            This perspective is really due to
            %a{:href => "#BrownSzczarba95"} Brown &amp; Szczarba 95
            (based on
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BrownSzczarba90"} Brown &amp; Szczarba 90
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BrownSzczarba93"} Brown &amp; Szczarba 93
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BrownSzczarba95Pages"} here
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+equivariant+Chern+character"} twisted equivariant Chern character
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+de+Rham+complex"} equivariant de Rham complex
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The notion of fiberwise
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rationalization"} rationalization
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#BousfieldKan72
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aldridge+Bousfield"}> Aldridge Bousfield
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Kan"} Daniel Kan
              = succeed "," do
                %em Homotopy Limits, Completions and Localizations
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-38117-4"} doi:10.1007/978-3-540-38117-4
          %p
            Rational homotopy theory (as well as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+homotopy+theory"} real homotopy theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and regarded as Borel-equivariant rational homotopy theory of their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+spaces"} covering spaces
            is laid out, and the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+theorem+of+dg-algebraic+rational+homotopy+theory"} fundamental theorem of dg-algebraic rational homotopy theory
            is proven, in:
          %ul
            %li#BrownSzczarba95
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edgar+Brown"}> Edgar Brown
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+H.+Szczarba"} Robert H. Szczarba
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Real and Rational Homotopy Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Algebraic+Topology"} Handbook of Algebraic Topology
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-044481779-2/50018-3"} doi:10.1016/B978-044481779-2/50018-3
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://mirrors.library.cornell.edu/ZMATH/zmath/en/zmath/search/?an=0865.55009"} ZB
              %ul
                %li#BrownSzczarba95Pages
                  see p. 883 &amp; 896 (16 &amp; 29 of 48):
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/BrownSzczarba_BorelEquivariantRHT.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p based on
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+Halperin"}> Stephen Halperin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Tanr%C3%A9"} Daniel Tanré
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Homotopie filtrée et fibres
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/journals/illinois-journal-of-mathematics/volume-34/issue-2/Homotopie-filtr%c3%a9e-et-fibres-Cinfty/10.1215/ijm/1255988268.full"} doi:10.1215/ijm/1255988268
            %li#BrownSzczarba90
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edgar+Brown"}> Edgar Brown
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+H.+Szczarba"} Robert H. Szczarba
                %em Continuous cohomology and Real homotopy type II
                Asterisque 191, Societe Mathematique De France (1990) (
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/book-part/AST_1990__191__45_0"}> numdam:AST_1990__191__45_0
                )
            %li#BrownSzczarba93
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edgar+Brown"}> Edgar Brown
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+H.+Szczarba"} Robert H. Szczarba
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Real and rational homotopy theory for spaces with arbitrary fundamental group
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/journals/duke-mathematical-journal/volume-71/issue-1/Rational-and-real-homotopy-theory-with-arbitrary-fundamental-groups/10.1215/S0012-7094-93-07111-6.short"} doi:10.1215/S0012-7094-93-07111-6
          %p with related discussion in
          %ul
            %li#GomezTatoHalperinTanre00
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Antonio+G%C3%B3mez-Tato"}> Antonio Gómez-Tato
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+Halperin"} Stephen Halperin
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Tanr%C3%A9"} Daniel Tanré
              = succeed "," do
                %em Rational homotopy theory for non-simply connected spaces
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9947-99-02463-0"} doi:10.1090/S0002-9947-99-02463-0
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/118074"} jstor:118074
          %p which is reviewed in:
          %ul
            %li#Ivanov22
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergei+O.+Ivanov"}> Sergei O. Ivanov
              , section 12 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em An Overview of Rationalization Theories of Non-simply Connected Spaces and Non-nilpotent Groups
              %strong 38
              (2022) 1705–1721 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.10694"}> arXiv:2111.10694
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10114-022-2063-9"} doi:10.1007/s10114-022-2063-9
          %p A textbook account is in:
          %ul
            %li#FelixHalperinThomas15
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yves+F%C3%A9lix"}> Yves Félix
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Halperin"} Steve Halperin
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Claude+Thomas"} Jean-Claude Thomas
              = succeed "," do
                %em Rational Homotopy Theory II
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/9473"} doi:10.1142/9473
          %blockquote
            %p
              (While
              %a{:href => "#FelixHalperinThomas15"} FHT 15
              does discuss, mostly in its Section 7, some aspects of non-simply connected spaces via the rational homotopy of their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+spaces"} covering spaces
              with attention to
              = succeed "-actions," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+theorem+of+dg-algebraic+rational+homotopy+theory"} fundamental theorem of dg-algebraic rational homotopy theory
              to this situation – nor does it cite any of the above.)
          %p Further discussion of fiberwise rational homotopy theory:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Manuel+Rivera"}> Manuel Rivera
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Felix+Wierstra"} Felix Wierstra
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mahmoud+Zeinalian"} Mahmoud Zeinalian
              = succeed "," do
                %em Rational homotopy equivalences and singular chains
              %strong 21
              (2021) 1535-1552 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.03655"}> arXiv:1906.03655
              \.
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2140/agt.2021.21.1535"} doi:10.2140/agt.2021.21.1535
        .revisedby
          %p
            Last revised on November 12, 2024 at 10:09:15.
